## Terragrunt 1.x (stargazer) CLI cheatsheet

The framework runs Terragrunt 1.x. Many legacy `--terragrunt-*` flags have
been renamed or removed. Use only what is below.

KNOWN-GOOD INVOCATIONS:
- `terragrunt run --all plan --non-interactive`
- `terragrunt run --all output --non-interactive`
- `terragrunt force-unlock <LOCK_ID>`        (positional id only — no `--force`)
- `terragrunt hcl format --working-dir <p>`  (replaces `hclfmt`)
- `terragrunt --working-dir <unit-dir> plan` (single-unit form)

PASSING TERRAFORM FLAGS:
Terraform-specific flags (`-out`, `-json`, `-lock=false`, `-refresh=false`)
MUST come after a literal `--` separator. Otherwise terragrunt rejects them.
  GOOD: `terragrunt run --all output --non-interactive -- -json`
  BAD:  `terragrunt run --all plan --non-interactive -out=foo`

FLAGS THAT NO LONGER EXIST (do not invent them):
- `--terragrunt-include-external-dependencies`
- `--terragrunt-non-interactive`     (just `--non-interactive`)
- `--terragrunt-parallelism`         (use `TG_PARALLELISM` env var)
- `--force` on `force-unlock`

IF YOU SEE `flag provided but not defined` or `is not a valid flag for ...`,
STOP. Re-read this cheatsheet. Do NOT retry the same command verbatim.

TIMEOUTS: init + plan across ~10 modules on a cold cache takes 5-15 minutes,
but local-model latency can stretch this further. Set `timeout_secs=6000` on
every shell call that runs terragrunt. If Goose rejects this value as too
large, fall back to `timeout_secs=3600` and report the rejection in your
next message — do not silently drop to a smaller number.

## Task

1. Confirm AWS identity:
   ```
   aws sts get-caller-identity
   ```
   Note the account id and ARN. Include both at the top of the summary file.

2. `cd {{iac_dir}}/live/{{env}}` and run plan:
   ```
   terragrunt run --all plan --non-interactive
   ```
   Tee the output to a file under `/tmp` so you can grep it later instead of
   re-running plan. Capture exit code.

3. Parse the plan stdout (NOT the source files) to extract per-unit
   `Plan: N to add, M to change, K to destroy.` lines. The format is:
     `[<unit>] terraform: Plan: N to add, M to change, K to destroy.`
   Build the summary table from these lines and these lines only.

4. **Greenfield handling.** On a clean account some downstream units (most
   commonly `ecs-service`) cannot be planned because their published Terraform
   module uses `data "aws_subnet"` / `data "aws_security_group"` lookups that
   query AWS for resources that do not yet exist. Symptoms:
   - `Error: no matching EC2 Subnet found`
   - `Error: no matching EC2 Security Group found`
   - errors referencing `data.aws_subnet`, `data.aws_security_group`, etc.
   This is **not** an IaC bug. List those units in the summary under a
   "Deferred to apply" section and continue. They will plan/apply correctly
   during the apply phase, after their dependencies are created.

5. **Real failures still stop you.** If a unit fails for any other reason
   (HCL syntax, missing module, IAM permission denied, S3 backend error,
   stale state lock that you could not clear, etc.), do NOT produce a success
   marker. Write `## Plan failed` at the top of the summary file with the
   verbatim error and stop.

6. Write `{{plan_summary_path}}` with this structure (Markdown):

   ```
   # Plan summary — {{repo_name}} / {{env}} / {{region}}

   - AWS account: <account>
   - AWS ARN:     <arn>
   - Generated:   <ISO8601 UTC timestamp>

   ## Per-unit plan
   | Unit | + add | ~ change | - destroy |
   |------|-------|----------|-----------|
   | vpc  |   19  |    0     |    0      |
   | ...  |       |          |           |

   ## Totals
   - To add:     <sum>
   - To change:  <sum>
   - To destroy: <sum>

   ## Notable changes
   - Any destroys (highlight loudly, especially for prod)
   - Any IAM, security group, or public-internet exposure changes
   - Any RDS / data-store changes (replacements, version bumps)

   ## Deferred to apply
   <units that legitimately could not plan on greenfield, with the exact error type>

   ## Plan stdout (truncated)
   <last ~50 lines of plan stdout, for traceability>
   ```

7. Print exactly one final line and stop:
   ```
   PLAN READY plan_summary={{plan_summary_path}}
   ```

## Constraints

- **Anti-hallucination — read carefully.** You are forbidden from filling
  the plan summary using anything other than real `terragrunt plan` stdout
  captured in this session. Specifically:
    - Do NOT read `.hcl`, `.tf`, `.tfvars`, `inputs.yaml`, or `env.hcl` to
      infer "what would be planned".
    - Do NOT generate counts, resource lists, ports, engines, or sizes from
      memory or by reading source files.
    - Do NOT write a Plan Summary if `terragrunt run --all plan` did not
      execute and produce output in this session.
    - If you find yourself listing resources that do not appear in the plan
      stdout file, STOP. That is hallucination.
  The summary's "Per-unit plan" rows MUST come from `Plan:` lines in the
  captured stdout. If a unit produced no `Plan:` line, list it under
  "Deferred to apply" with the verbatim error excerpt — do not estimate.

- **No mutations.** Do not run `apply`, `destroy`, `import`, `state rm`,
  `taint`, or any command that changes AWS or terraform state. The only
  state-touching command allowed is `terragrunt force-unlock <id>` to clear
  a clearly stale lock owned by this same machine.

- Output language: **English only**.

- If plan succeeds (or finishes with only "Deferred to apply" units), print
  `PLAN READY plan_summary={{plan_summary_path}}` and stop.

- If plan fails for any other reason, do NOT print `PLAN READY`. Write the
  failure to the summary file with the verbatim error and stop.
